Parses a decimal integer from a string: an optional minus sign, then one or more digits. The result is a pair: [0 n] for a number, or [1 message] for a failure, with a message that says what was wrong. A failure is a value, not a raise, so a caller can check it and carry on.
The probe is errors as values. A minus sign alone, and an empty string, are both failures. The tests check the message as part of the output.
---
---
(lam s
  (letrec digits (lam l (lam acc
        (if (? l) [0 acc]
            (let c (head l)
              (if (< c 48) [1 "not a number"]
                  (if (< 57 c) [1 "not a number"]
                      ((digits (tail l)) (+ (* acc 10) (- c 48)))))))))
    (if (? s) [1 "empty"]
        (if (= (head s) 45)
            (if (? (tail s)) [1 "no digits"]
                (let r ((digits (tail s)) 0)
                  (if (= (head r) 0) [0 (- 0 (tail r))] r)))
            ((digits s) 0)))))
---
"42" => [0 42] fuel 200000
"-123" => [0 -123] fuel 200000
"0" => [0 0] fuel 200000
"007" => [0 7] fuel 200000
"99999" => [0 99999] fuel 200000
"-0" => [0 0] fuel 200000
"" => [1 "empty"] fuel 200000
"-" => [1 "no digits"] fuel 200000
"12a" => [1 "not a number"] fuel 200000
"1 2" => [1 "not a number"] fuel 200000
"+5" => [1 "not a number"] fuel 200000
